@hostAddress = https://localhost:7205
# With plain `dotnet run` (profile "http") only HTTP is served:
# @hostAddress = http://localhost:5053

### Import catalog (multipart/form-data)
# The multipart body needs an explicit boundary, and the file is attached with `< path`
# (relative to this .http file). `file=@path` is curl syntax and is not understood here.
POST {{hostAddress}}/api/catalog/imports
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----CatalogBoundary
Accept: application/json

------CatalogBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="ProductEntry.json"
Content-Type: application/json

< ../load-tests/ProductEntry.json
------CatalogBoundary--

### Import choosing the matching rule (README decision #11)
# "name" ignores the brand: this row links to the catalog's "Router WiFi 6 TP-Link" (TP-Link)
# and the report says so in nameOnlyMatches plus a warning.
POST {{hostAddress}}/api/catalog/imports
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----CatalogBoundary
Accept: application/json

------CatalogBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="import.json"
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "matchStrategy": "name",
  "products": [
    {
      "Id": "aaaaaaaa-1111-4111-8111-111111111111",
      "SellerName": "NameSeller",
      "Name": "Router WiFi 6 TP-Link",
      "Brand": "Netgear",
      "Category": "Networking"
    }
  ]
}
------CatalogBoundary--

###
